# Resync the system clock from the DS3231, periodically, from USERLAND.
#
# RUN IT AS "sh /etc/rtcsync", NOT "/etc/rtcsync".  There is deliberately
# no #! line here, because one would be a lie on this machine: the
# kernel does not parse the interpreter out of it (syscall_execelf32.c,
# CONFIG_SCRIPT_INTERP) - it runs the platform's FIXED interpreter,
# which is /usr/bin/bcrun, so that compiled BASIC and C run as ./prog.
# A "#!/bin/sh" script therefore execs bcrun and dies with "not a
# bytecode object", and a shebang here would just invite that.
#
# The kernel used to do this itself: updatetod() called plt_rtc_secs()
# from the timer interrupt and that did a whole I2C transaction, about
# hourly.  It cannot stay there once userland can drive the same bus -
# the QWIIC connector is on that controller - because an interrupt can
# land in the middle of a user transaction and interrupt context can
# neither take a lock nor wait for one.  So plt_rtc_secs() returns 255
# now and the correction happens here, in process context.
#
# Not cron: the V7 cron on this card does setuid(1) before running
# anything, and setting the clock needs root.  This is smaller anyway.
#
# setdate -a reads the chip and sets the clock without ever prompting -
# the plain form falls back to asking on the terminal, which would wedge
# a background job.  A failure is ignored on purpose: a board with no
# RTC fitted, or a QWIIC device holding the bus down, should not fill
# the console with complaints once an hour.
#
# The correction is a STEP, where the kernel's was a continuous slide.
# Between steps the clock is as good as the RP2350's crystal, a few
# seconds a day.  Nothing on this machine cares, and anything that did
# would want a real time source rather than a better slide.

while :
do
	sleep 3600
	setdate -a 2>/dev/null
done
